WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:02.801
-Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy-winning writer

00:00:02.801 --> 00:00:05.267
and an Emmy- and
Golden Globe-nominated actor.

00:00:05.267 --> 00:00:08.267
His children's book, "Zilot
&amp; Other Important Rhymes,"

00:00:08.267 --> 00:00:09.100
is on sale now.

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:10.067
Please welcome back to the show,

00:00:10.067 --> 00:00:11.767
our friend Bob Odenkirk,
everybody.

00:00:11.767 --> 00:00:14.767
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:14.767 --> 00:00:21.267
♪♪

00:00:21.267 --> 00:00:27.734
♪♪

00:00:27.734 --> 00:00:29.000
Welcome back, Bob.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:30.634
-Thank you, man.
Great to be here.

00:00:30.634 --> 00:00:33.000
-You are a SAG member.
You're a WGA member.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:35.200
You were very active
on the picket lines this summer.

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:37.067
-I really was.
That's your job, right?

00:00:37.067 --> 00:00:38.801
-That is your job.
And you did a great job.

00:00:38.801 --> 00:00:40.400
-Well, I --
This is my third strike.

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:42.501
-Congratulations.
-Thank you.

00:00:42.501 --> 00:00:45.100
[ Chuckles ]
Badge of honor.

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:47.934
I -- You know,
I know what to do.

00:00:47.934 --> 00:00:50.868
You go out to where
the other writers are

00:00:50.868 --> 00:00:53.667
and you walk in circles,
and that's what you do.

00:00:53.667 --> 00:00:55.067
And that's your part
of the job.

00:00:55.067 --> 00:00:57.601
-Did you find that
as you get older,

00:00:57.601 --> 00:01:00.934
you realize that if you walk in
the same direction for too long,

00:01:00.934 --> 00:01:03.033
one hip just starts to hurt?
-Yes. [ Laughs ]

00:01:03.033 --> 00:01:06.234
-Yeah. I really -- I'm like,
"Guys, we got to switch it up.

00:01:06.234 --> 00:01:08.167
This is -- We're going to be
feeling this tomorrow."

00:01:08.167 --> 00:01:11.467
-That's right. And if you
turn around and go the other way

00:01:11.467 --> 00:01:14.534
or you just switch people
that you're with,

00:01:14.534 --> 00:01:17.968
then you get other
screenplays...from them.

00:01:17.968 --> 00:01:19.367
-Do you like the camaraderie
of the picket line?

00:01:19.367 --> 00:01:20.801
-I love the camaraderie.
-It's really nice.

00:01:20.801 --> 00:01:25.167
I mean, writers kind of
are loners, sort of.

00:01:25.167 --> 00:01:26.934
They kind of are in their lives.

00:01:26.934 --> 00:01:28.234
But then you put them in a line

00:01:28.234 --> 00:01:31.167
and give them something to hate
together,

00:01:31.167 --> 00:01:35.133
they become best buds,
and we're all chums.

00:01:35.133 --> 00:01:37.234
-You're a pro now,
but did you feel like a rookie

00:01:37.234 --> 00:01:40.734
in the '88 strike?
-Oh, I was such an ass[bleep].

00:01:40.734 --> 00:01:43.601
-Uh-huh.
-I was such a jerk.

00:01:43.601 --> 00:01:45.834
Like, I had been hired
at "Saturday Night Live"

00:01:45.834 --> 00:01:49.834
only a few shows
before the strike.

00:01:49.834 --> 00:01:53.501
What right did I have?
[ Chuckles ]

00:01:53.501 --> 00:01:57.200
And -- And I go to the strike
with my friend Robert Smigel.

00:01:57.200 --> 00:01:58.901
-Fantastic writer.
-One of the great

00:01:58.901 --> 00:02:00.567
writers of all time.

00:02:00.567 --> 00:02:04.167
And we get there
and somebody is speaking.

00:02:04.167 --> 00:02:07.934
Somebody is giving a speech.
"We can do this! Hang in there!"

00:02:07.934 --> 00:02:10.501
Some old guy is going on and on.

00:02:10.501 --> 00:02:12.067
And I'm like,
"Who's this ass[bleep]?

00:02:12.067 --> 00:02:14.133
This guy's a jerk.
Shut up already.

00:02:14.133 --> 00:02:15.467
Let's start walking."

00:02:15.467 --> 00:02:17.534
Somebody leans over
to me and goes,

00:02:17.534 --> 00:02:19.834
"That's Budd Schulberg" --

00:02:19.834 --> 00:02:23.601
one of the great writers
of the century.

00:02:23.601 --> 00:02:27.567
And here I am,
this jerk from Naperville,

00:02:27.567 --> 00:02:28.801
you know, giving him [bleep].

00:02:28.801 --> 00:02:32.934
But I felt duly chastised,
and --

00:02:32.934 --> 00:02:35.133
and I carried on with the job.

00:02:35.133 --> 00:02:38.467
-You talked about
your early "SNL" time,

00:02:38.467 --> 00:02:41.267
and you wrote a great book
about your career in comedy.

00:02:41.267 --> 00:02:43.067
-Oh, thank you.
-And I heard you recently

00:02:43.067 --> 00:02:44.901
tell the story,
and this actually has

00:02:44.901 --> 00:02:47.701
some lore
about talking to a host,

00:02:47.701 --> 00:02:49.067
because sometimes
you have to talk

00:02:49.067 --> 00:02:51.367
to really accomplished actors.
-This was a great actor.

00:02:51.367 --> 00:02:53.767
Jeremy Irons.
-I mean, to this day.

00:02:53.767 --> 00:02:56.133
-One of the greats.
-And what was your --

00:02:56.133 --> 00:02:57.300
what was your --
-My experience

00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:01.300
was that I had written
a monologue for him

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:03.367
with someone else,
another writer.

00:03:03.367 --> 00:03:06.067
And it was -- It took us,
you know,

00:03:06.067 --> 00:03:08.934
a good 26 minutes to write it.

00:03:08.934 --> 00:03:12.167
A lot of work went into it,
and it's his monologue

00:03:12.167 --> 00:03:13.667
and he's been waiting
to do the show.

00:03:13.667 --> 00:03:17.567
And he was so mad.
And he's reading it like this.

00:03:17.567 --> 00:03:19.267
And I'm sitting right here.
He's right here.

00:03:19.267 --> 00:03:20.968
His face is right here,

00:03:20.968 --> 00:03:23.467
and he's looking at this thing
and he's going,

00:03:23.467 --> 00:03:27.300
"You're making me do this?
I mean..."

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:29.734
And he starts complaining

00:03:29.734 --> 00:03:32.834
and I have my recorder
and I press record,

00:03:32.834 --> 00:03:34.534
and I get this monologue.

00:03:34.534 --> 00:03:36.834
Which camera have I got?

00:03:36.834 --> 00:03:42.934
"I mean, I can dance,
I can sing,

00:03:42.934 --> 00:03:45.267
I can do Shakespeare,

00:03:45.267 --> 00:03:46.801
I can juggle,

00:03:46.801 --> 00:03:48.467
I can do anything,

00:03:48.467 --> 00:03:50.934
and you have me do this?!"
[ Laughter ]

00:03:50.934 --> 00:03:55.434
And, oh, it was the greatest.
It was so great. I loved it.

00:03:55.434 --> 00:03:57.434
And then he had
to do that monologue.

00:03:57.434 --> 00:04:00.167
[ Laughter ]

00:04:00.167 --> 00:04:02.167
He did.

00:04:02.167 --> 00:04:04.701
-I like -- Does that mean
you went back to Lorne's office,

00:04:04.701 --> 00:04:05.834
and he's like,
"Does he like it?"

00:04:05.834 --> 00:04:06.801
And you're like,
"He's good to go."

00:04:06.801 --> 00:04:09.367
-"He loves it! He's on board."

00:04:09.367 --> 00:04:11.334
-So, this is fantastic.

00:04:11.334 --> 00:04:12.767
This is a book that has been,

00:04:12.767 --> 00:04:14.534
I mean, two decades
in the making.

00:04:14.534 --> 00:04:15.968
-It is. It is.

00:04:15.968 --> 00:04:19.067
Zilot is a word
invented by my son Nate

00:04:19.067 --> 00:04:22.634
when he was about six years old
for a blanket fort.

00:04:22.634 --> 00:04:25.067
Okay?
You build blanket forts, right?

00:04:25.067 --> 00:04:26.934
-Yeah, of course.
-With kids, they love it.

00:04:26.934 --> 00:04:29.601
And you put a blanket
over a chair and a couch,

00:04:29.601 --> 00:04:30.834
get inside, read books,

00:04:30.834 --> 00:04:33.167
and the cat comes in
and it's the best.

00:04:33.167 --> 00:04:34.667
And...

00:04:34.667 --> 00:04:37.467
And my kids, we would read
a lot of books before bedtime.

00:04:37.467 --> 00:04:38.667
And at a certain point, I said,

00:04:38.667 --> 00:04:41.234
"Let's start
writing some poems."

00:04:41.234 --> 00:04:44.601
And so we wrote a bunch
of really silly poems

00:04:44.601 --> 00:04:46.200
and I kept them on a shelf.

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:49.133
And then 15 years later,
here comes a pandemic.

00:04:49.133 --> 00:04:50.968
My daughter is an artist.

00:04:50.968 --> 00:04:53.434
-So she drew all the pictures.
-She did all the illustrations,

00:04:53.434 --> 00:04:55.601
and they're really beautiful.
-She did a wonderful job.

00:04:55.601 --> 00:05:00.067
-Yeah. And we just sat down
and rewrote the poems

00:05:00.067 --> 00:05:02.234
so they were good, because...
[ Laughter ]

00:05:02.234 --> 00:05:04.734
-So you do have the original --
-I have the original book.

00:05:04.734 --> 00:05:07.167
-This is what you kept on a
shelf, which is very impressive.

00:05:07.167 --> 00:05:08.434
-This is what
I kept on the shelf.

00:05:08.434 --> 00:05:10.501
-Yeah.
-"Old Time Rhymes" I called it.

00:05:10.501 --> 00:05:13.667
I wanted the kids to feel like
they had really written a book.

00:05:13.667 --> 00:05:15.400
By Dad, Nate and Erin.

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:18.234
And the truth is, look,
there were --

00:05:18.234 --> 00:05:21.300
I knew there were, like,
10 good ones in here,

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:23.701
but most of the time
they're like this.

00:05:23.701 --> 00:05:25.467
And here's one I just
picked out for you just

00:05:25.467 --> 00:05:29.000
to show you my kids were not
born magically delicious.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:31.667
[ Laughter ]
This one's called "Stay up."

00:05:31.667 --> 00:05:34.133
[ Clears throat ]
"Eat some jello.

00:05:34.133 --> 00:05:35.300
Go play the cello."

00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:37.701
See those...?
[ Laughter ]

00:05:37.701 --> 00:05:41.300
"Go out on the street
and meet an odd fellow.

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:44.567
Put a splash of toothpaste
in your comb if you dare,

00:05:44.567 --> 00:05:48.501
so you can clean its teeth
while you fix your hair."

00:05:48.501 --> 00:05:50.234
-Not terrible.
-Not terrible.

00:05:50.234 --> 00:05:52.934
-Not terrible.
-Kind of...

00:05:52.934 --> 00:05:56.100
Kind of clunky.
-So you obviously come back.

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:57.701
So it's pandemic.
You got the kids in the house.

00:05:57.701 --> 00:06:00.701
-So I'm rewriting
one or two or three a week

00:06:00.701 --> 00:06:02.267
and I'm knocking on
my daughter's door

00:06:02.267 --> 00:06:05.601
like it's a, you know,
"30 Rock," an office.

00:06:05.601 --> 00:06:08.434
"Where are those drawings?
Let's go.

00:06:08.434 --> 00:06:10.701
You said you'd
have them yesterday!

00:06:10.701 --> 00:06:11.901
Ahh."
-And what about Nate?

00:06:11.901 --> 00:06:13.767
Was Nate sort of
getting off scot free?

00:06:13.767 --> 00:06:16.534
-No, he. -- My son. I was like,
"I need 10 more poems."

00:06:16.534 --> 00:06:18.701
-[ Laughs ]
When they were that age,

00:06:18.701 --> 00:06:20.734
when they were writing poems
with you back then,

00:06:20.734 --> 00:06:22.000
were they aware
that you were funny?

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:22.901
Were they entertained by you?

00:06:22.901 --> 00:06:25.267
-Um...yeah.
-Okay.

00:06:25.267 --> 00:06:27.801
-I was trying to be funny.

00:06:27.801 --> 00:06:29.734
I was very silly
around the kids.

00:06:29.734 --> 00:06:34.167
Really fun to do that.
And they kind of wised up.

00:06:34.167 --> 00:06:36.767
They loved my silliness.
-Right.

00:06:36.767 --> 00:06:40.200
-But one time when my daughter
was six, it was so great.

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:42.701
I was doing a character
in the mirror.

00:06:42.701 --> 00:06:44.567
"Yeah, yeah, yeah,
time to go to school!

00:06:44.567 --> 00:06:47.567
Let's go! Get on the bus!
Put on your clothes!"

00:06:47.567 --> 00:06:50.300
And -- And I stopped,
and she goes...

00:06:50.300 --> 00:06:51.400
"Bad."

00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:54.334
[ Laughter ]

00:06:54.334 --> 00:06:57.667
Six year old. You know?
I loved it.

00:06:57.667 --> 00:06:59.234
-And you started
your recorder again.

00:06:59.234 --> 00:07:00.734
You're like...
-"Come on."

00:07:00.734 --> 00:07:03.334
-"I'd like to get this on tape."
-"I got a monologue for you."

00:07:03.334 --> 00:07:07.033
-I do know that there are
a lot of kids books these days.

00:07:07.033 --> 00:07:09.567
I would imagine it's a very
tough market to sell one.

00:07:09.567 --> 00:07:10.567
-Well, usually it is.

00:07:10.567 --> 00:07:12.934
But this month
we got very lucky.

00:07:12.934 --> 00:07:16.267
All the other kids books
coming out are just terrible.

00:07:16.267 --> 00:07:17.968
[ Laughter ]
-Now...

00:07:17.968 --> 00:07:19.868
we didn't believe you
when we heard...

00:07:19.868 --> 00:07:22.801
-Yeah, they're awful.
-And so we went and --

00:07:22.801 --> 00:07:24.567
And you had mentioned --
-And you bought a few.

00:07:24.567 --> 00:07:27.100
You just randomly bought --
-I couldn't believe

00:07:27.100 --> 00:07:28.601
that these were actual titles,

00:07:28.601 --> 00:07:30.267
because you did hit
the jackpot that your book --

00:07:30.267 --> 00:07:32.300
-I just got lucky.
I just got lucky.

00:07:32.300 --> 00:07:34.133
-Like, here's -- here's
a new book that is out.

00:07:34.133 --> 00:07:35.501
-This one's coming out.
-It's called

00:07:35.501 --> 00:07:38.067
"Nothing But Sharp Edges."
[ Laughter ]

00:07:38.067 --> 00:07:39.767
-Why would you make this?
-I mean, show --

00:07:39.767 --> 00:07:41.033
Do you want
to show them what...?

00:07:41.033 --> 00:07:43.334
-Why would you make
this book for children?

00:07:43.334 --> 00:07:45.968
Why? Why would you...?

00:07:45.968 --> 00:07:48.400
Why?
-That's crazy!

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:49.601
-Why would you...?

00:07:49.601 --> 00:07:52.200
This is not -- This is...

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:53.501
This is not...
-I mean, yeah,

00:07:53.501 --> 00:07:56.801
if it's like, look,
even if I don't like poems,

00:07:56.801 --> 00:07:58.000
like, it's a no-brainer.

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:00.801
-I'd go with the poetry book.
-And then this...

00:08:00.801 --> 00:08:04.634
And, again, I understand there's
a lot of books about, like,

00:08:04.634 --> 00:08:06.834
going to the bathroom
is a hard thing for kids to get.

00:08:06.834 --> 00:08:09.934
So I'm not judging that it's
about going to the bathroom.

00:08:09.934 --> 00:08:13.901
But "That's right, Esme,
the Toilet Has Teeth."

00:08:13.901 --> 00:08:16.534
-Why would you teach...?
Why would you show kids...?

00:08:16.534 --> 00:08:19.267
That's not a thing
that you want to do.

00:08:19.267 --> 00:08:20.701
-Yeah!
I would think this would --

00:08:20.701 --> 00:08:22.667
-This is our competition.
-Yeah.

00:08:22.667 --> 00:08:24.400
-We're golden.
-What a good month.

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:26.834
-Yes.
-And then sometimes, like,

00:08:26.834 --> 00:08:31.267
there's, like, promotional
tie-ins with film or TV.

00:08:31.267 --> 00:08:32.734
-Sure.
-And I think that pays off.

00:08:32.734 --> 00:08:34.934
You know, if there's a big kids
movie coming out and, like,

00:08:34.934 --> 00:08:37.133
you get a book and, you know,
the kids get to see it.

00:08:37.133 --> 00:08:40.200
But I can't imagine that
there's any parent out there

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:45.534
that wants to get their kid
"Li'l Oppenheimer

00:08:45.534 --> 00:08:47.567
and His Big Booming Bomb."

00:08:47.567 --> 00:08:49.167
-Why would you want
to give the kid that?

00:08:49.167 --> 00:08:52.133
That's not a good thing
for the kid to have!

00:08:52.133 --> 00:08:53.467
-It's not a good thing for kids!

00:08:53.467 --> 00:08:55.234
[ Both speaking shrilly ]

00:08:55.234 --> 00:08:56.501
-Go to the last page of this.

00:08:56.501 --> 00:08:58.033
-I don't even want to,
because I am like,

00:08:58.033 --> 00:08:59.934
"How do you even teach kids...?"
-This is for children.

00:08:59.934 --> 00:09:01.367
-No!
[ Laughter ]

00:09:01.367 --> 00:09:02.667
You're telling me this is

00:09:02.667 --> 00:09:04.534
the last page of this book?
-Show them.

00:09:04.534 --> 00:09:05.968
-I mean, I will,
but I'm worried.

00:09:05.968 --> 00:09:08.367
-"...and everybody will die.

00:09:08.367 --> 00:09:10.133
Everybody.

00:09:10.133 --> 00:09:14.167
The end."
-I mean, what? Come on.

00:09:14.167 --> 00:09:16.200
I mean, it's "Zilot"
or nothing this month.

00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:18.000
This month,
it's "Zilot" or nothing.

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:19.667
You guys, Bob Odenkirk!

00:09:19.667 --> 00:09:21.901
[ Cheers and applause ]
"Zilot" is on sale now.

00:09:21.901 --> 00:09:24.634
We'll be right back with "SNL's"
Marcello Hernandez.

